Process

	1. SSL&TLS Basics
		1.1 Symmetric encryption
			I. Same key is used at both encrypting and decrypting the data
		1.2 Asymmetric encryption
			I. A pair of keys, public and private exists in this encryption
			II. To give a concrete example, public key could be imagined as a public lock and private key still a regular key. Anyone can see the public lock(key) but only the owner would have the private key.
			III. ssh-keygen binary is used for creating private keys and public locks.
				- Then we can add the public lock to the target server's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys file 
				- We can now use our private key to access to the target server.
		1.3 Certificates
			I. We can securely communicate with servers using asymmetric and symmetric encryption but we cannot rule out the possibility that the server we are communiticating with is malicious.
			II. To ascertain that we are communicating with the server we intend to communicate, domain names are imprinted on the certificates and browsers checks whether the target server's domain name and the certificate it has provided aligns
			III. Web browsers tells us if the certificates is secure and valid or not
			IV. We create certificate signing request and private key then we send the csr to the CA(Certificate Authorities) CA's also have public and private keys. CA's sign the csr we have sent it and returns it to us. Browsers are able to verify the certificates signed by CA's since their public key is available to the internet/public
			V. This whole process is named public key infrastructure
			VI. public key/certificate's extensions are crt and pem, private key's extensions are key and pem
